% The Café Paradox — the barber paradox in catering form.
%
% A town's inhabitants divide into those who live in a household that cooks
% its own meals and those who don't.  Policy: anyone who does NOT live in a
% household that cooks may eat free in the café.  The café is operated by
% one of the households (cafe_house), and Bob — a member of that household —
% is assigned to cook the meals served in the café.
%
% Modelling note: this encoding reads "a household cooks its own meals" as
% a statement about the meals its members actually take, so whether a
% household cooks depends on where its members eat (second household_cooks
% rule below).  That reading is what turns Bob's situation into a cycle
% through negation.  The alternative, direct reading — Bob cooks the café's
% meals, the café is his household, therefore his household cooks, full
% stop — is encoded in cafe_constraint.dl, where the same paradox surfaces
% instead as an integrity violation in a stratified program.
%
% Evaluate with --models for the semantic verdict: this program has NO
% stable model (Bob's atoms form an odd loop through negation, the shape of
% "p :- not p"), and its well-founded model leaves exactly Bob's three
% atoms undefined.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------- facts
member(alice, house_a).
member(alan,  house_a).
member(carol, house_b).
member(bob,   cafe_house).

cooks_at_home(alice).        % alice cooks house_a's meals at home
cooks_cafe_meals(bob).       % bob cooks the meals served in the café

% ------------------------------------------------------------------- rules
% A household cooks its own meals if a member cooks at home ...
household_cooks(H) :- member(P, H), cooks_at_home(P).

% ... or if its members take their meals in the café and the café's meals
% are cooked by a member of that very household.  (Bob's situation: the
% meals his household eats in the café are cooked by Bob himself.)
household_cooks(H) :-
    member(P, H), eats_in_cafe(P), cooks_cafe_meals(Cook), member(Cook, H).

% Everyone whose household cooks eats at home.
eats_at_home(P) :- member(P, H), household_cooks(H).

% Everyone whose household does not cook may eat free in the café.
eats_in_cafe(P) :- member(P, H), not household_cooks(H).
